  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nUpper level pattern consists of a shortwave diving through \nMississippi River Valley towards the Ohio and Tennessee Valley \nregion. A warm front lifting through the CWA has been the driving \nforce for showers and a few thunderstorms this morning and early \nafternoon. While a few isolated showers can be noted behind the main \nrain shield, some clearing is present across portions of central \nTennessee into northern Alabama and along the Cumberland Plateau. A \ntrailing cold front is expected to progress through the region this \nevening, with additional shower and storm development along and \nahead of this front. There are still some discrepancies among CAMs \nin how well organized or the exact coverage area of convection along \nthis feature, however, one trend that catches my eye is that of the \nNAMs.\n\nLatest surface observations show temperatures rebounding into the \nlow to mid 80s in the southern valley and plateau, upper 70s on the \nnorthern plateau, but remaining in the low 70s in portions of the \ncentral valley and eastward. The NAM seems to depict a trend in \nstorm coverage that best aligns with where precip has lingered \nlongest this afternoon... one where storms that develop over middle \nTennessee persist into the Plateau, but as the activity progresses \nfurther east into the CWA, the focus is generally along and south of \nI-40 where better destabilization occurs among higher theta-e \nadvection. \n\nOverall, HREF depicts around 1500-2000 J/kg of MLCAPE and effective \nshear between 20 and 25kts as this activity is expected to take \nplace. Additionally, PWAT will be well into the 90th percentile. \nThis will lead to damaging winds and isolated flooding as the \nprimary hazards. However, the shear is just strong enough that a low \ntornado risk will is in place, primarily along the Cumberland \nPlateau and southern Valley where the greatest destabilization has \noccurred. Of course any storm that has enough rotation for a \npotential tornado could also exhibit small hail as well. Activity \nwill gradually trend downward in intensity around midnight or \nshortly thereafter. \n\nAs we head into Sunday afternoon, a few additional showers and \nstorms may develop along the slowly progressing cold front. Latest \ntrend in CAMs favors far northeast Tennessee and southwest Virginia \nfor additional convective initiation. Strong effective shear and \nincreasing DCAPEs will support at least a isolated wind damage \nand flooding threat where storms train along the boundary. \nDepending on how much clearing takes place and resulting CAPE \nprofiles, cannot rule out some small hail as well. \n\nAn upper low is expected to be cutoff as we head into the new work \nweek, which will continue to promote daily chances of showers and \nstorms among slightly below normal temperatures. Some\ndeterministic models begin to eject the low late week, with a \ndownward trend in precip chances and slight warming trend.",
